<script>
let count = 0;

/* bloom::event */
function count_incremented(): number {
    return count;
}

/* bloom::command */
function increment_count (by: number) {
    count += by;
    count_incremented();
}

/* bloom::reactive */
function count_is_even (): bool {
    return count % 2 === 0;
}

function count_is_odd (): bool {
    return count % 2 === 1;
}

</script>

<vstack>
  <text>Count: {count}</text>
  <text>Is Even: {count_is_even()}</text>
  <text>Is Odd: {count_is_odd()}</text>
  <button click:{() => increment_count(1)}}>Increment!</button>
<vstack>

<!--
  We parse the script tag.

  1) Collect all variables declared using let, along with their types. These become the candidates for the state set
     It is mandatory that variables are strongly typed unless declared. Type inference will be done only for builtin
     types. Objects must be strongly typed. Stray objects are dissallowed.
  2) Collect all functions that are events, commands and reactive. Resolve their types. For functions, it is made 
     mandatory that both the parameters AND the returns are strongly typed.
  3) The script is then transformed into something like:
  ```
    let ctxt = {
      count: $state(0),

      /* bloom::event */
      count_incremented() {
        // code that sends the event goes here.
        const process = () => {
          // anything that went inside the actual
          // count_incremented function goes here
          return count.v;
        }

        event.send(id, "count_incremented", process()) // something along these lines
      }

      /* bloom::command */
      increment_count (by) {
          // this entire function is kept as is
          // the workspace resolves the right ids
          // and will invoke this function
          count.v += by;
          count.update();
          count_incremented();
      }

      /* bloom::reactive */
      count_is_even: {
        value: () => {
          // anything that went inside the actual
          // count_is_even function goes here
          return count.v % 2 === 0;
        }
        deps: [count]
      }

      count_is_odd (){
          // this is just a regular function
          return count.v % 2 === 1;
      }
    }
  ```

  4) this context is then used by the actual widget, which may look like this:
  ```
    function build_counter () {
      let id = $acquire(); // acquire an id from the pool
      let ctxt = ...;
      
      const w = new Widget();

      w.build = ($) => {
        // <vstack>
        const w1 = build_vstack();

       // <text>Count: {count}</text>
        const w2 = build_text();
        w2.setInner(() => {
          return `Count: ${$.count.v}`
        }, [$.count]);
        w1.add($w2);

       // <text>Is Even: {count_is_even()}</text>
        const w3 = build_text();
        w3.setInner(() => {
          return `Is Even: ${$.count_is_even.value()}`
        }, [$.count_is_even.deps]);
        w1.add(w3);

       // <text>Is Odd: {count_is_odd()}</text>
        const w4 = build_text();
        w3.setInner(() => {
          return `Is Odd: ${$.count_is_odd())}`
        }, []);
        w1.add(w4);

       // <button click:{() => increment_count(1)}}>Increment!</button>
        const w5 = build_button();
        w5.binds("click", () => increment_count(1))
        w5.setInner(() => {
          Increment!
        }, [])
        w1.add(w5);
      }

      w.ctxt = ctxt;

      $register(w); // register the widget
      return w;
    }
  ```
-->

quick doubt. in this svelte code:

<script>
	let name = $state('world');
	let count = $state(0);
</script>

<h1>Hello {(()=>{return name})()}!</h1>

<input bind:value={name} />
<button onclick={() => count += 1}>
	clicks: {count}
</button>

when we do {(()=>{return name})()}, the entire thing is still reactive. is this a js consequence because return name means return the reference to name? 
this is what the output in the browser looks like when i don't call the closure. i can see name gets replaced with get(name). so what does this semantically mean?
